{{also|Mingle}}
==English==
{{Webster 1913}}

===Etymology===
From earlier {{m|enm|mingil}}, {{m|en|mengle}}, from {{inh|en|enm|menglen}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|ming|le}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|mengen||to mingle, mix}}, {{cog|de|mengen||to mingle, mix}}. More at {{l|en|ming}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmɪŋ.ɡəl/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-mingle.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪŋɡəl|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[intermix]]; to [[combine]] or [[join]], as an individual or part, with other parts, but commonly so as to be distinguishable in the product.<ref name=Webster>{{R:Webster 1913}}</ref>
#: {{syn|en|confuse|confound}}
#* {{RQ:Tyndale Pentateuch|book=Exodus|chapter=9|verse=24|folio=XV|verso=1|passage=[T]here was hayle ãd fyre '''mẽgled''' with the hayle, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Tupper Proverbial Philosophy|poem=Of Searching for Pride|page=69|passage={{smallcaps|Be}} aware of the smiling enemy, that openly sheatheth his weapon, / But '''mingleth''' poison in secret with the sacred salt of hospitality.}}
#* {{RQ:NYT|author=w:Sheryl Gay Stolberg|title=Bush Mourns 9/11 at Ground Zero as N.Y. Remembers|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20110926114926/https://www.nytimes.com/2006/09/11/nyregion/nyregionspecial3/11bush.html|date=11 September 2006|passage=Across the city yesterday, there was a feeling of bittersweet reunion as streams of humanity converged and mingled at dozens of memorial services.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To associate or unite in a figurative way, or by ties of relationship.
# To cause or allow to intermarry.
# To intermarry.
#* {{RQ:Coverdale Bible|1 Esdras|8|87|folio=vij|verso=1|column=2|passage=[W]e haue b&#42843;okẽ thy ſtatutes ⁊ cõmaundementes agayne, ⁊ '''mengled''' o{{sup|&#42843;}} ſelues w{{sup|t}} the vnclẽnes of the outlandiſh heithen.|translation=[W]e have broken thy statutes and commandments again, and '''mingled''' ourselves with the uncleanness of the foreign heathen.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Ezra|9|2|page=589|column=1|passage=[T]he holy ſeed haue '''mingled''' themſelues with the people of ''thoſe'' lands, {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To deprive of purity by mixture; to [[contaminate]].<ref name=Webster></ref>
#* {{quote-text|en|year=a. 1729|author={{w|John Rogers (priest)|John Rogers}}|title=The Necessity of Universal Obedience
|passage=a '''mingled''', imperfect virtue}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make or prepare by mixing the ingredients of.<ref name=Webster></ref>
#* {{RQ:Hawthorne Scarlet Letter|chapter=The Interview|page=86|passage=The physician {{...}} proceeded to '''mingle''' another draught.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To put together; to join.<ref name=Webster></ref>
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Cymbeline|act=I|scene=vi|page=375|column=2|passage=Some dozen Romanes of vs, and your Lord <br>(The beſt Feather of our wing) haue '''mingled''' ſummes <br>To buy a Preſent for the Emperor: {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become mixed or blended.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To socialize with different people at a social event.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2009|author=Jane Buckingham|title=The Modern Girl's Guide to Life
|passage=And allow a bit of a cocktail hour before the meal so that when your guests arrive, you have time to '''mingle''' before you step into the kitchen.}}

